new file mode 100644
index 0000000..74dc9b9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/_docstrings/objects.Perc.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+By default, `Perc` computes quartiles and the endpoints of the observed data:
+
+.. plot::
+
+    import pandas as pd
+    import seaborn.objects as so
+    data = pd.DataFrame({"x": ["a"] * 4, "y": [1, 2, 3, 4]})
+    so.Plot(data, x="x", y="y").add(so.Dots(), so.Perc())
+
+Pass an integer to request that many evenly spaced levels from 0 through 100,
+or pass explicit percentile levels:
+
+.. plot::
+
+    import pandas as pd
+    import seaborn.objects as so
+    data = pd.DataFrame({"x": ["a"] * 4, "y": [1, 2, 3, 4]})
+    so.Plot(data, x="x", y="y").add(so.Dots(), so.Perc(3))
+
+.. plot::
+
+    import pandas as pd
+    import seaborn.objects as so
+    data = pd.DataFrame({"x": ["a"] * 4, "y": [1, 2, 3, 4]})
+    so.Plot(data, x="x", y="y").add(so.Dots(), so.Perc([10, 50, 90]))
+
+Combine `Perc` with a range mark to show an interval:
+
+.. plot::
+
+    import pandas as pd
+    import seaborn.objects as so
+    data = pd.DataFrame({"x": ["a"] * 4, "y": [1, 2, 3, 4]})
+    so.Plot(data, x="x", y="y").add(so.Range(), so.Perc([25, 75]))
